course: politics
lesson: 37
title: School councils and class reps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A class rep is a pupil chosen by the class to speak for it at the school council, a small parliament of pupils that meets with teachers to make school better.
explain: Running is like a mini election: you are a candidate, you give a short speech, and the best promises are ones you can really try to keep, like asking for more football time, not "free sweets every Friday".
explain: Fair voting needs a secret ballot, slips of paper in a box, so nobody votes just to please a best friend, and no lollies for votes, because that is a bribe.
explain: The council has a budget, a fixed amount of money, so it must choose; a good rep listens to the teachers' reasons too and carries messages both ways.
explain: Losing hurts, but a fair vote is still fair; a good meeting has a chair who makes room for every voice, even the youngest and quietest.
fact: A class rep speaks for the class at the school council; a person trying to get elected is called a candidate.
fact: A campaign promise is something a candidate says they will do; promising what you cannot make happen makes people cross later.
fact: A short speech with three clear ideas said slowly works better than lots of fast ones, and grown-up politicians do the same.
fact: When people can see how you vote they may vote to please friends, which is why real elections use a secret ballot.
fact: Accepting that you lost a fair vote is called conceding, and you can still share your ideas afterwards.
fact: A budget is a plan for how to spend a fixed amount of money, so a council must choose a priority, like a playground or a library.
fact: A representative speaks for a group and also brings back what happened, including the other side's reasons.
fact: Some countries, such as New Zealand, have youth parliaments where young people debate in the real chamber and send ideas to politicians, though their votes do not make laws.
fact: When a promise fails, explaining honestly what you tried and why it did not work is called accountability.
fact: Giving gifts for votes is a bribe, and in real elections paying or gifting people for votes is against the law in most countries.
fact: A survey asks lots of people the same question; a referendum is when a whole country votes on one big question.
fact: A chair runs a meeting and decides who speaks next, and an agenda is the list of things the meeting will talk about.
fact: Words in this lesson: "rizz" means charm; "sound" in Liverpool means nice or fine; "grand" in Ireland means fine; "bostin'" in the Black Country means brilliant and "ta" means thanks; "Maccies" is British slang for McDonald's.
exercise: What does a class rep do? | Speaks for the class at the school council | Runs the school instead of the head teacher | Marks everyone's homework every single evening | Decides the school menu all alone, every week
exercise: Sam's mate says, "Just promise free sweets every Friday." Why is that a bad promise? | Sam cannot make it happen, so people would be cross later | Sweets are against the law in every country, so the promise would get the whole class into trouble | Promises are not allowed in class votes | Nobody in the class likes sweets
exercise: What is a candidate? | A person trying to get elected | A person who counts all the votes | A sweet given out before a vote | The person who runs the meeting
exercise: What tip does Zara's big sister give for her speech? | Tell them three things you'd do | Speak as fast as you possibly can | Tell lots of jokes and give no ideas | Read the whole school rules aloud
exercise: In a show of hands, some kids look to see what their best friend does. What fixes this? | A secret ballot with paper slips in a box | Voting twice to be sure | Letting the teacher choose for everyone, so that nobody has to watch their friends | Only letting best friends vote
exercise: Grace loses by three votes and says it isn't fair because Tyler is funny. What is true? | A fair vote is still fair even if you lose | The vote should be done again until she wins | Funny people are not allowed to win | Three votes is not enough to win
exercise: What is conceding? | Accepting that you lost and the other person won | Refusing to accept the result | Asking for the vote to be done again | Giving sweets to get votes
exercise: The head teacher has money for only a playground or a library makeover. What is this about? | A budget: choosing because money is limited | A bribe: buying votes with books | A referendum on the whole country | A protest outside the town hall
exercise: Mateo's class wants phones at lunch, and at council he hears the teachers' reasons for the ban. What should he do? | Tell the class what he asked, why they said no, and what might be allowed | Tell the class the teachers agreed when they didn't, so that nobody in the class is disappointed with him | Keep the teachers' reasons secret | Quit being class rep straight away
exercise: What is a youth parliament? | Young people debating issues and sending ideas to real politicians | A parliament where children make the country's laws, which the grown-ups then have to obey | A playground game with no rules | A group that runs the school canteen
exercise: Dylan promised pizza on Fridays but the kitchen said no because of menu rules. What is the honest thing to do? | Explain what he tried and why it did not work | Pretend he never promised it and hope everyone forgets about it | Blame his classmates for asking | Promise even bigger things next time
exercise: Two kids hand out lollipops saying "Vote for me, have a lolly." What is that? | A bribe | A survey | A speech | A referendum
exercise: How can Finn's council find out what every pupil thinks about the uniform? | A quick paper survey in every class | Ask only the loudest pupils in each class | Let the council decide without asking anyone | Ask one pupil and guess what the rest think
exercise: What is a referendum? | A vote where everyone answers one big question | A list of things a meeting will talk about | A letter to a member of parliament | A gift given to win votes
exercise: The older boys talk over Amira and she can't get a word in. What can help? | A chair who gives everyone a turn | Letting the loudest person win | Stopping all meetings forever | Telling Amira to stop coming
test: "just promise free Maccies every day, it's got mad rizz, everyone'll vote for ya" — what is the best reply? | Only promise what you can really try to do | Great idea, big promises always work, and nobody checks them after the vote | Promises don't matter after you win | Promise even more food to be safe
test: In Liverpool: "Sound speech, but you said 'um' like fifty times, la." What tip would help? | Pick three clear ideas and say them slowly | Talk faster so the ums disappear | Say nothing and just smile | Read the dictionary aloud instead
test: What would happen if a class voted by hands up and everyone watched their best mate first? | Some would vote to please friends, not for what they think | Everyone would vote exactly what they really think, because watching your mates helps you decide | The vote would become a secret ballot | Nobody would be allowed to vote
test: "It's not fair, he only won cos he's funny, I'm gonna sulk all week" — what is a better way to lose? | Congratulate the winner and keep sharing your ideas | Ask the teacher to cancel the result | Tell everyone the vote was cheating | Never speak at class meetings again
test: In Dublin: "Ah, grand, we'll get the playground AND the library AND a pool!" The budget covers one thing. What is wrong? | A fixed budget means choosing one priority | Councils can always spend as much as they want | Pools are free for every school | Libraries cost nothing to fix
test: "You lied! No pizza!" Dylan really did ask, but the menu rules said no. What is fairest? | He did not lie if he tried, but he should explain honestly | He lied, because every promise that does not come true is a lie, whatever the reason for it | He should hide from the class | He should get the pizza by sneaking in
test: In the Black Country: "Bostin' lollies, ta, I'll vote for ya!" What is wrong here? | Gifts for votes are a bribe, so the vote is about lollies not ideas | Nothing, it is just being friendly | Lollies are the only fair way to campaign, as long as every candidate hands out the same number | Bribes are fine if the sweets are small
test: In a cartoon, the loudest animal always speaks at the meeting and the little mouse never gets a turn. What would fix it? | A chair who decides who speaks next | A louder mouse | More loud animals | Ending all meetings
test: A boy says, "Youth parliaments make the real laws, so I can ban homework." What is the better version? | Their votes do not make laws, but leaders can hear their ideas | He is right, they make all the laws, and the real parliament has to sign whatever they decide | Youth parliaments only exist in cartoons | Youth parliaments are against the law
test: "class rep just goes to the meetings and never tells us nothing" — what is missing? | A good rep brings back what happened | A good rep keeps everything secret | Reps should only talk to teachers | Reps are not allowed to report back
test: In Toronto: "Survey? Nah, just ask the five loudest kids, eh, quicker." What is the problem? | Asking only the loud ones misses the quiet voices | The five loudest always know what everyone thinks | Surveys are against school rules | Quiet kids do not care about uniforms
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the function of a class representative? | To represent the class before the school council and to report back upon its proceedings | To assume the duties of the head teacher during meetings of the council and whenever the head teacher is absent | To determine the school menu without consultation | To assess the homework of fellow pupils
exam: Identify the principal reason for which elections are conducted by secret ballot. | So that each voter may choose freely, without pressure from those observing | So that the count may be concluded more slowly | So that the candidates may not cast a vote | So that only acquaintances of the candidates may vote, others being unable to see the ballot paper
exam: It is given that a candidate distributes confectionery in exchange for an undertaking to vote for him. State the term for such conduct. | Bribery | Conceding | A referendum | A survey
exam: Whereas the funds available suffice for one undertaking only, select the response most consistent with the proper conduct of the council. | The council determines a priority after hearing the views advanced on each side | The council undertakes both projects regardless of the funds, borrowing whatever further sums may be required | The council declines to consider either project | The council permits the loudest member to decide alone
